<section class="card">
    <img src="{{ @IMG . 'content-kinder-scout.jpg' }}">
    <div>
        <h3>Kinder Scout</h3>
        <p>
            Kinder Scout is the highest point in the Peak District, rising to 636 meters (2,087 feet).
            This dramatic moorland plateau offers breathtaking panoramic views, wild landscapes, and a rich history of access rights and conservation.
            As a key feature of many hiking routes, including the Pennine Way, Kinder Scout remains a beloved destination for walkers, 
            nature lovers, and history enthusiasts alike.
        </p>
        <p>
            The name "Kinder" dates back to the Domesday Survey of 1086, where it was recorded as Chendre.
            The exact meaning of the name is obscure, though it is believed to be of pre-English origin, 
            suggesting that it has ancient roots predating the Anglo-Saxon period. 
            The word "Scout" comes from the Old Norse word skúte, which means a high, overhanging rock. 
            This is a fitting description for the cliffs on the western side of Kinder Scout, 
            which dominate the landscape and offer dramatic views across the Peak District. 
            These cliffs have long attracted adventurers and explorers due to their rugged beauty and challenging terrain.
        </p>
        <p>
            This blend of ancient languages reflects the long-standing connection between people and this landscape, 
            from the pre-English settlers to the Norse influences that have shaped the place names of Northern England.
        </p>        
    </div>
</section>

<section class="card">
    <img src="{{ @IMG . 'content-mass-trespass.jpg' }}">
    <div>
        <h3>Kinder Mass Trespass</h3>
        <p>
            On 24 April 1932, Kinder Scout became the site of a pivotal moment in British history - the Kinder Mass Trespass.
            This was a coordinated act of civil disobedience organized by Benny Rothman to protest the denial of public access to areas of open countryside.
            Ramblers, who were frequently expelled from private land, defied the law by marching onto the privately owned moorland of Kinder Scout.
        </p>
        <p>
            This event not only led to several arrests but also significantly raised awareness of the need for public access to the countryside.
            The trespass is widely credited with influencing the passage of the National Parks legislation in 1949.
            This, in turn, led to the creation of the Peak District as the UK's first National Park in April 1951.
            Other national parks such as the Lake District, Snowdonia, and Dartmoor were designated in the same year.
            Moreover, it helped pave the way for the establishment of long-distance footpaths like the Pennine Way.
        </p>
        <p>
            The Kinder Mass Trespass wasn't just a stand-alone protest but the culmination of a broader movement stretching back over many years, 
            where ordinary people used trespass as a way to reclaim the countryside for the public good. 
            The tradition of trespass continued to influence access rights, culminating in modern legislation such as the Countryside and Rights of Way Act (CROW) in 2000,
            which enshrined the "right to roam" in many areas of England and Wales.
        </p>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="card">
    <img src="{{ @IMG . 'content-kinder-woolpacks.jpg' }}">
    <div>
        <h3>Wonders and Wildlife</h3>
        <p>
            Aside from its historical significance, Kinder Scout is a nature lover's dream. 
            The plateau is home to a diverse range of wildlife and boasts unique geological formations shaped by centuries of weathering.

            For example, Kinder Downfall is the Peak District's tallest waterfall at 30 meters (98 feet). 
            On windy days, the water is often blown back on itself, creating a dramatic upward spray.
            On the southern edges of Kinder Scout are The Woolpacks, a fascinating area with surreal rock formations that resemble boulders stacked by giants.

            Kinder Scout is also home to an array of wildlife, including red grouse, mountain hares, and even peregrine falcons.
            Conservation efforts have led to the reintroduction of native plants such as sphagnum moss, helping to restore the blanket bog habitat that had been eroded over time.
        </p>
    </div>
</section>


